Then, the problem says: Let DP and BC meet at F. So, we need to consider the lines DP and BC. DP is the line from D to P, and BC is the line from B to C. Their intersection is point F, and we have to prove that F is the midpoint of BC.

Alright, so to prove that F is the midpoint of BC, that would mean that BF = FC, or equivalently, that F divides BC into two equal parts.

Given all that, I think the best way to approach this is to assign coordinates to the points so that we can do the problem algebraically. Let me set up a coordinate system.

